{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794769085,"nonce":"LBjWQXpC2hy3IYr4sVMoklbs1mQ=","title":"An opacity or colour cycle that repeats draws at 30 frames a second, not the display's rate","message":"With only a repeating opacity or colour animation visible (the cn indeterminate bar, skeletons) and nothing else moving, the window drew about 95 frames a second, each a cache frame of about 0.6 ms (BLINC_MOTION_TRACE, cn demo, owner's display). The engine already says such animations \"tolerate the cap fine\" (`has_visible_vsync_class`), but the demo sets no cap, so the cap never applied.\n\n`animate.rs` now draws at 30 frames a second while the only motion is a CSS opacity or colour cycle: nothing vsync class is visible, no scroll, theme animation, flow, image fade, pointer query, animating stateful, and no binding that moves anything but opacity (a binding carrying a CSS fade reads as moving for as long as it plays, so it is excluded when it carries opacity alone). A rebuild, relayout, scroll or vsync-class animation draws at once. Trace: ambient_only and ambient_terms say which term held it back.\n\nMeasured: drawn frames 95 a second to 27 to 28; the owner reads the idle bar at 4.9% CPU from 10% (13 to 20% in earlier readings).\n\nCosts: a frame an input event asks for while the cap is on can wait up to 33 ms. Watch hover on a page with a bar visible. Part of what P3 (29e250d) set out to do, from the other side: fewer frames rather than cheaper ones.\n\nLabels: perf, area:app.","files":null}]}